gs to the set \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  How many points of \\(T_n\\) can a non‑sunny line contain?\n\n*Horizontal line.*  \nA line of the form \\(y=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+c\\le n+1\\).  \nFor a fixed \\(c\\) there is at most one integer \\(a\\) satisfying both conditions, namely \\(a=n+1-c\\) (if this integer is \\(\\ge 1\\)).  \nHence a horizontal line contains **at most one** point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Vertical line.*  \nThe same argument shows that a line \\(x=c\\) also contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Diagonal line \\(x+y=d\\).*  \nIf a line has equation \\(x+y=d\\) (i.e. slope \\(-1\\)), then any point of \\(T_n\\) on it must satisfy \\(a+b=d\\).  \nSince the definition of \\(T_n\\) requires \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), there is at most one point of \\(T_n\\) with a given sum \\(d\\).  \nThus a diagonal line also contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nConsequently **any non‑sunny line covers at most one point of \\(T_n\\).** \\hfill\\(\\square\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Counting the points covered by non‑sunny lines\n\nSuppose we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(s\\) be the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\); then the number of non‑sunny lines equals \\(n-s\\).\n\nBy the observation of §1, the non‑sunny lines together can cover at most \\(n-s\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  \nTherefore the sunny lines must cover at least\n\n\\[\n|T_n|-(n-s)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-(n-s) \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA sunny line can have any slope other than \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nSuch a line can intersect the triangle \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\