) in at most \\(n\\) lattice points (the maximal number occurs when the line passes through the points \\((1,m), (2,m-1),\\dots,(m,1)\\) for some \\(m\\le n\\)).  \nHence each sunny line can cover **at most \\(n\\)** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nFrom (2) we obtain the inequality\n\n\\[\ns\\cdot n \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}-(n-s),\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n\\[\nsn \\ge \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-n+s\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nsn - s \\ge \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-n\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\ns(n-1) \\ge \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}-n .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(n-1>0\\) gives\n\n\\[\ns \\ge \\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,}\n= \\frac{n}{2}-\\left(1+\\frac{1}{n-1}\\right)\n= \\frac{n-2}{2}-\\frac{1}{n-1}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(s\\) is an integer, we obtain the clean bound\n\n\\[\ns \\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Consequence\n\nEquation (3) shows that **any admissible configuration must contain at least**\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil}\n\\]\n**sunny lines.**  \n\nThus the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid k\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\Bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Remarks on sharpness\n\nThe bound (3) is attained for several small values of \\(n\\) (for instance \\(n=3,4\\)) by explicit constructions using a mixture of horizontal, vertical and diagonal lines together with a minimal number of sunny lines.  \nHowever, a complete classification of all attainable \\(k\\) for arbitrary \\(n\\) remains open.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**End of Detailed Solution**"
