            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Number of points to be covered\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) contributes exactly \\(s-1\\) points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Points covered by a non‑sunny line\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\):  \nA point \\((a,c)\\in T_n\\) must satisfy \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+c\\le n+1\\).  \nIf such an \\(a\\) exists it is uniquely determined as \\(a=n+1-c\\).  \nThus at most one point of \\(T_n\\) lies on a given horizontal line.\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\): the same reasoning shows at most one point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=d\\) (slope \\(-1\\)): any point of \\(T_n\\) on it satisfies \\(a+b=d\\).  \nSince the sum \\(a+b\\) is fixed, there is at most one admissible lattice point.  \n\nConsequently **any non‑sunny line contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).** \\hfill\\(\\square\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Points covered by a sunny line\n\nLet a sunny line have equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIntersecting this line with the region \\(x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\) yields a line segment whose integer points satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx\\ge 1,\\\\\nmx+b\\ge 1,\\\\\nx+mx+b\\le n+1.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nThe third inequality can be rewritten as \\((1+m)x\\le n+1-b\\).  \nSince \\(1+m\\neq0\\) and \\(m\\neq -1\\), the length of the segment in the \\(x\\)-direction is at most \\(n\\).  \nTherefore the number of integer points on the line is at most \\(n\\).  \n(Indeed, taking \\(m=-\\frac{p}{q}\\) with coprime positive integers \\(p,q\\) and choosing \\(b\\) appropriately yields a line passing through the points \\((1,m_1), (2,m_2),\\dots,(m,1)\\) for any \\(m\\le n\\), showing the bound \\(n\\) is sharp.)\n\nHence **each sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).** \\hfill\\(\\square\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Deriving a lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines; then \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nFrom §§ 2 and 3 the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\cdot n+(n-k)\\cdot 1 = kn + n - k .\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) must be covered, we require  \n\n\\[\nkn + n - k \\;\\ge\\; |T_n| = \\frac{(n-1)n}{2}.\n\\]\n\nRearranging,\n\n\\[\nk(n-1) \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} - n\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk \\;\\ge\\; \\frac{n}{2} - \\frac{n}{\\,n-1\\,}\n= \\frac{n-2}{2} - \\frac{1}{n-1}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is an integer, the inequality yields  \n\n\\[\nk \\;\\ge\\; \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nEquation (2) shows that any admissible configuration of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil}\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  \nTherefore the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is contained in  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid k\\ge\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe bound is sharp for several small values of \\(n\\) (e.g. \\(n=3,4\\)), but a complete description of all attainable \\(k\\) for arbitrary \\(n\\) remains open.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
