            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Number of points to be covered\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) contributes exactly \\(s-1\\) points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. How many points can a single line contain?\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if its slope belongs to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); otherwise it is **sunny**.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \n- A horizontal line \\(y=c\\) meets \\(T_n\\) in the set \\(\\{(a,c)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;a+c\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n  The inequality forces \\(a=n+1-c\\); therefore at most one admissible point lies on the line.  \n- The same argument works for a vertical line \\(x=c\\).  \n- A line of slope \\(-1\\) has equation \\(x+y=d\\).  The condition \\(a+b=d\\) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(d\\le n+1\\); for a fixed \\(d\\) there is at most one lattice point of \\(T_n\\).  \n\nThus **any non‑sunny line contains at most one point of \\(T_n\\).** \\hfill\\(\\square\\)\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nLet a sunny line be given by \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \nIntersect it with the triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_n=\\{(x,y)\\mid x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe intersection is a line segment whose projection on the \\(x\\)-axis has length at most \\(n\\) (because the endpoints satisfy \\(x\\ge1\\) and \\(x\\le n\\)). Consequently the segment can contain at most \\(n\\) integer points.  \n\nMoreover the bound \\(n\\) is attainable: for any integer \\(m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n\\) the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_m:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{m-1}\\,x + m\n\\]\n\npasses through the \\(m\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,m-1),\\;(2,m-2),\\dots ,\\;(m-1,1)\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(T_n\\). Hence a sunny line can contain up to \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\). \\hfill\\(\\square\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. A necessary lower bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\).  \nLet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines; then \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n\nFrom the previous paragraph:\n\n* each non‑sunny line contributes at most **one** point of \\(T_n\\);\n* each sunny line contributes at most **\\(n\\)** points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTherefore the total number of points that can be covered satisfies  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)\\cdot 1 + k\\cdot n \\;\\ge\\; |T_n| .\n\\]\n\nUsing (3) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nn-k+kn \\ge \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\nk\\ge \\frac{n-1}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is integral,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\ge \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\,}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Trivial upper bound\n\nObviously we cannot have more sunny lines than the total number of lines, so  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le n\\,}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Realising every integer \\(k\\) with \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nk_0=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\n#### 5.1 Construction for \\(k=k_0\\)\n\nDefine the following \\(k_0\\) sunny lines (all of slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\)):\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,k_0 .\n\\]\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k_0-1\\}\\) **iff** it lies on one of the lines \\(L_i\\).  \nA direct count shows that the set  \n\n\\[\nU:=T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{k_0}L_i\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n-k_0\\) points. Moreover, any two distinct points of \\(U\\) have distinct\n\\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(x+y\\); consequently each point of \\(U\\) can be assigned a different non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) that passes through it and through no other point of \\(U\\).\n\nBecause we have precisely \\(n-k_0\\) non‑sunny lines available, we can cover all points of \\(U\\).  \nThus we obtain a family of \\(n\\) lines with exactly \\(k_0\\) sunny ones that covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 5.2 Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(k_0<k\\le n\\)\n\nStart from the configuration for \\(k_0\\).  \nReplace arbitrarily \\(t=k-k_0\\) of the non‑sunny lines by sunny lines of a new slope (for instance slope \\(2\\)).  \nWhen a non‑sunny line that previously covered a single point \\(P\\) is replaced, we let the new sunny line pass through \\(P\\); because a sunny line can contain up to \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\), we may also let it pass through any other still‑uncovered points (if any).  \n\nAfter the replacement we still have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, now with \\(k\\) sunny ones, and every point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered.  \n\nHence a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4)–(5).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Final answer\n\nCombining the necessary condition (4), the trivial upper bound (5) and the explicit constructions of §5, we conclude that for a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\left\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\;\\big|\\;\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\right\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is completely solved.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
