proving that all\n   such values are attainable.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The only restriction on the number of sunny lines is the trivial bound\n   \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Both the necessity (from (1)) and the sufficiency (explicit\n   construction) have been established, completing the solution.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Number of points to be covered\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) contributes\n\\(s-1\\) lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. How many points can a single line contain?\n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be any line.\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\)*, then \\(\\ell\\) is of the form \\(x+y=c\\) with\n\\(c\\le n+1\\). The condition \\(a+b=c\\) together with \\(a,b\\ge1\\) yields at most\none point of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\).\n\n*If \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(m\\neq -1\\)*, write it as \\(y=mx+b\\).  \nIntersect \\(\\ell\\) with the triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_n=\\{(x,y)\\mid x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe intersection is a line segment whose projection on the \\(x\\)-axis is an\ninterval of length at most \\(n\\) (the extreme \\(x\\)-coordinates are\n\\(1\\) and at most \\(n\\)). Consequently the segment can contain at most \\(n\\)\ninteger points, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell\\cap T_n|\\le n . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe bound \\(n\\) is attainable. For any integer \\(m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n\\) the\nline  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_m:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{m-1}x+m\n\\]\n\npasses through the \\(m\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,m-1),\\;(2,m-2),\\dots ,\\;(m,1),\n\\]\n\nall of which belong to \\(T_n\\). Thus (3) is sharp.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Necessary condition on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every point of \\(T_n\\), and let\n\\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.  \nEach line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) by (3); therefore the total\ncapacity of the family is at most \\(n\\cdot n=n^{2}\\). Since (2) gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}<n^{2}\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\nthe capacity condition imposes **no restriction** on \\(k\\).  The only obvious\nrestriction is that we cannot have more sunny lines than the total number of\nlines:\n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) there exists a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of\n\\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 4.1 A base configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the diagonal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) such lines, they are pairwise distinct, and each point\n\\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\); hence it lies on the\ncorresponding \\(\\ell_s\\).  Thus the set \\(\\{\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\}\\) is a\nvalid configuration with **no** sunny lines.\n\n#### 4.2 Introducing sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nFrom the base configuration choose arbitrarily \\(k\\) of the diagonal lines,\nsay \\(\\ell_{s_1},\\dots ,\\ell_{s_k}\\), and replace each of them by a sunny line\nof slope \\(1\\) (any slope different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\) would work).  \n\nFor a fixed \\(s\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nS_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(s-1\\le n\\) points.  Because a sunny line can contain up to\n\\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\), we can choose a sunny line of slope \\(1\\) that passes\nthrough **all** points of \\(S_s\\).  The \\(k\\) new sunny lines are distinct\nbecause they have different intercepts (the sums \\(s_i\\) are distinct).\n\nThe remaining \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines are left unchanged, so the total number\nof lines is still \\(n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of\n\\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines (either on its original diagonal\nline or on the newly introduced sunny line).\n\nThus a configuration exists for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (4) and the explicit constructions of §4 together show\nthat **all** integers \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n\n\\]\n\nare attainable, and no other values are possible. Hence the set of all\nnon‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required family of \\(n\\) lines\nexists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
