            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Number of points to be covered\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) the equation \\(a+b=s\\) contributes\n\\(s-1\\) lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. How many points can a single line contain?\n\n*Diagonal lines.*  \nA line of slope \\(-1\\) has the form \\(x+y=c\\).  \nIf \\(c\\le n+1\\) then the points of \\(T_n\\) on this line are exactly the\n\\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=c\\) and \\(a,b\\ge1\\); there are \\(c-1\\) of them.\nIn particular the line \\(x+y=2\\) contains the single point \\((1,1)\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line has slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\) and can be written\n\\(y=mx+b\\). Its intersection with the triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_n=\\{(x,y)\\mid x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nis a line segment whose projection on the \\(x\\)-axis has length at most \\(n\\);\nconsequently it can contain at most \\(n\\) integer points of \\(T_n\\).  \nMoreover, for any fixed sum \\(s\\ge 3\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nS_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains at least two points, and these points are **not** collinear on a line\nwith slope different from \\(-1\\). Hence a sunny line can never contain the whole\nset \\(S_s\\) when \\(s\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Necessary restriction on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and that\nexactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nIf \\(k\\ge 2\\), then at least two of the diagonal lines \\(x+y=s\\) would have to\nbe removed (otherwise we would have more than \\(n\\) lines).  \nFor any \\(s\\ge 3\\) the points of \\(S_s\\) cannot be covered by a single sunny\nline, as shown above. The only diagonal line that can be replaced by a sunny\nline is \\(x+y=2\\), which contains the single point \\((1,1)\\). Therefore at most\none diagonal line can be substituted, giving  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nSince obviously \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), (3) yields the necessary condition  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Constructions attaining the two admissible values\n\n#### 4.1. \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) diagonal (non‑sunny) lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\; x+y=s ,\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThey are distinct, none is sunny, and each point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies\n\\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\); hence it lies on \\(\\ell_s\\).  Thus a configuration\nwith \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n#### 4.2. \\(k=1\\)\n\nReplace the line \\(\\ell_2\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=2\\), which contains only the point\n\\((1,1)\\)) by any sunny line passing through \\((1,1)\\); for definiteness let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell^{\\ast}:\\; y = x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\) has slope \\(1\\), so it is sunny.  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines \\(\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) are kept.\nNow we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely one of which is sunny, and\nevery point of \\(T_n\\) is still covered (the point \\((1,1)\\) is now on\n\\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\)).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (4) and the explicit constructions for the two values\nshow that the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
