qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThey are distinct, none is sunny, and each \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the unique\ndiagonal with sum \\(a+b\\).  Thus a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nReplace the diagonal \\(\\ell_2\\) (which contains only \\((1,1)\\)) by any sunny\nline through \\((1,1)\\); for example  \n\n\\[\n\\ell^{\\ast}:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\) has slope \\(1\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\), so it is sunny.\nAll other diagonals \\(\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) are retained.  We now have\nexactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely one of which is sunny, and every point\nof \\(T_n\\) is still covered (the point \\((1,1)\\) is on \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\)).  \n\nThus configurations exist for both admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition \\(k\\le 1\\) together with the explicit constructions for\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) proves that the only possible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
