            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the *diagonal*\n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\n|D_s|=s-1,\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n-1)}2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope \\(m\\) satisfies \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1,\\infty\\}\\).\nThen \\(\\ell\\) meets each diagonal \\(D_s\\) in at most one point; consequently\n\\(\\ell\\) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\neq-1\\).  Intersecting with the\ndiagonal equation \\(x+y=s\\) gives \\(x+mx+b=s\\), which has a unique solution for\n\\(x\\) (and hence for the point) unless the two lines coincide, which would require\n\\(m=-1\\).  Thus at most one point of \\(D_s\\) lies on \\(\\ell\\).  Since there are\n\\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\) and the point \\((1,1)\\) belongs to\n\\(D_2\\), the total number of points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) is at most \\(n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n### Lemma 2  \nA horizontal line (\\(y=c\\)), a vertical line (\\(x=c\\)), or a diagonal line\n(\\(x+y=c\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  For a diagonal the claim follows from \\(|D_c|=c-1\\le n\\).\nFor a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) the admissible points are \\((a,c)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); the number of such integers \\(a\\) is \\(n+1-c\\le n\\).\nThe vertical case is symmetric. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Main Argument\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\)\ncovering every point of \\(T_n\\) and that exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\nLet  \n\n\\[\nd=n-k\n\\]\n\nbe the number of non‑sunny lines.\n\nBy Lemma 2 a non‑sunny line that is not a diagonal can cover at most the same\nnumber of points as a diagonal, and replacing such a line by a suitable diagonal\ndoes not decrease the set of covered points.  Hence we may, without loss of\ngenerality, assume that all \\(d\\) non‑sunny lines are distinct diagonals; denote\nthem by  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{s_1},\\dots ,\\ell_{s_d}\\qquad\\text{with }\\ell_{s}=D_s .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### Omitted diagonals\n\nIf a diagonal \\(D_s\\) is **absent** from the above list, its \\(s-1\\) points must be\ncovered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines.  By Lemma 1 each sunny line contributes at most\none point on \\(D_s\\); therefore\n\n\\[\ns-1\\le k. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus any omitted diagonal satisfies \\(s\\le k+1\\).  Since exactly \\(d=k\\) diagonals\nare omitted, the set of omitted diagonals is forced to be\n\n\\[\n\\{D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### The diagonal \\(D_{k+2}\\)\n\nThe diagonal \\(D_{k+2}\\) contains \\(k+1\\) points.  The \\(k\\) sunny lines can\ncover at most \\(k\\) of them (one per line), leaving at least one point of\n\\(D_{k+2}\\) uncovered.  The only lines capable of covering points of \\(D_{k+2}\\)\nare the non‑sunny lines, but by (3) all non‑sunny lines are the diagonals\n\\(D_2,\\dots ,D_{k+1}\\); none of them equals \\(D_{k+2}\\).  Hence the remaining\npoint of \\(D_{k+2}\\) cannot be covered at all – a contradiction.\n\nTherefore the assumption \\(k\\ge 2\\) is impossible, and we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(k\\) is a non‑negative integer, the only possibilities are\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions showing attainability\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s,\\qquad s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThey are distinct, none is sunny, and each \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on the unique\ndiagonal with sum \\(a+b\\).  Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n*Case \\(k=1\\).*  \nStart from the family above and replace the diagonal \\(\\ell_2\\) (which contains\nonly the point \\((1,1)\\)) by any sunny line through \\((1,1)\\); for example  \n\n\\[\n\\ell^{\\ast}:\\;y=x .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\) has slope \\(1\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\), so it is sunny.  All other\ndiagonals \\(\\ell_3,\\dots ,\\ell_{n+1}\\) are retained.  Thus we have exactly \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines, precisely one of which is sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) is\nstill covered (the point \\((1,1)\\) is now on \\(\\ell^{\\ast}\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition forces \\(k\\le 1\\); the explicit constructions realise\n\\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\).  Consequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines\nfor the given problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
