2013 Lemma\u202f2 gives the universal bound \\(k\\le 1\\); the two explicit families show that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable. Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nV_c=\\{(c,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-c\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{c=1}^{n}V_c\\quad\\text{and the sets }V_c\\text{ are pairwise disjoint}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a\\) is a positive integer not exceeding \\(n\\) (otherwise \\(a\\ge n+1\\) would give \\(a+b>n+1\\)). Hence \\((a,b)\\in V_a\\). Conversely, any \\((c,b)\\in V_c\\) satisfies \\(c+b\\le c+(n+1-c)=n+1\\), so \\((c,b)\\in S_n\\). Distinct values of \\(c\\) give distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, therefore the \\(V_c\\) are disjoint. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f2 (necessity of vertical lines)\n\nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers every point of \\(S_n\\),\n\n* at least \\(n-1\\) of the lines are vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* consequently the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies \\(k\\le 1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix a column \\(V_c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\).\n\n*If a line \\(\\ell\\) is not vertical*, then its equation can be written as \\(y=m x+b\\) with finite slope \\(m\\). All points of \\(V_c\\) have the same abscissa \\(x=c\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) can intersect the vertical line \\(x=c\\) in **at most one** point. Hence a non\u2011vertical line can contain at most one element of \\(V_c\\).\n\nFor \\(c\\le n-1\\) we have \\(|V_c|=n+1-c\\ge 2\\) (by Lemma\u202f1). Since a non\u2011vertical line can cover at most one point of such a column, the two (or more) points of \\(V_c\\) cannot all be covered by non\u2011vertical lines. Thus **some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that contains a point of \\(V_c\\) must be vertical**. Because the columns \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are disjoint, the vertical lines required for different columns are distinct. Hence at least \\(n-1\\) distinct vertical lines belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nA vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so it is **not** sunny. Since \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-(n-1)=1 .\n\\qquad\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\). All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny. This yields a family with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(S_n\\) except those with abscissa \\(x=n\\). From the definition of \\(S_n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\{(n,b)\\in S_n\\}=\\{(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(n+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le 1\\) and \\(b\\) is a positive integer.  \n\nAdd a line \\(\\ell\\) through \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y=-\\tfrac12\\,(x-n)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1},\\ell\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly one sunny line and, by construction, covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nLemma\u202f2 shows that any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines can contain at most one sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le 1\\). The two explicit constructions demonstrate that both \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable. Therefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]"
