r more) points of \\(V_c\\) cannot all be covered by non\u2011vertical lines. Thus **some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) that contains a point of \\(V_c\\) must be vertical**. Because the columns \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1}\\) are disjoint, the vertical lines required for different columns are distinct. Hence at least \\(n-1\\) distinct vertical lines belong to \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nA vertical line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so it is **not** sunny. Since \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-(n-1)=1 .\n\\qquad\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\). All lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence none is sunny. This yields a family with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,2,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point of \\(S_n\\) except those with abscissa \\(x=n\\). From the definition of \\(S_n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\{(n,b)\\in S_n\\}=\\{(n,1)\\},\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(n+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b\\le 1\\) and \\(b\\) is a positive integer.  \n\nAdd a line \\(\\ell\\) through \\((n,1)\\) whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); for instance  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; y=-\\tfrac12\\,(x-n)+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), from \\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)). Hence \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1},\\ell\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly one sunny line and, by construction, co